The problem asks us to calculate the sum of $f(P)$ over all permutations $P$ of $(1, 2, \ldots, N)$, modulo 998244353.
Here, $f(A)$ is formed by concatenating the decimal representations of the elements of $A$.
Let $A = (A_1, A_2, \ldots, A_N)$. Then $f(A)$ is the integer formed by concatenating $A_1, A_2, \ldots, A_N$.
Specifically, if $L_i$ is the number of digits in $A_i$, then $f(A) = \sum_{i=1}^N A_i \cdot 10^{\sum_{j=i+1}^N L_j}$.
Wait, the definition says "Interpret S as a decimal integer".
Let's look at the example $A=(1, 20, 34)$.
$S$ starts empty.
$i=1, A_1=1$. $T="1"$. $S="1"$.
$i=2, A_2=20$. $T="20"$. $S="120"$.
$i=3, A_3=34$. $T="34"$. $S="12034"$.
$f(A) = 12034$.
This can be written as $1 \cdot 10^4 + 20 \cdot 10^2 + 34 \cdot 10^0$.
Generally, if $A_i$ has $L_i$ digits, then $A_i$ contributes to the sum as $A_i \cdot 10^{S_{i+1}}$, where $S_{i+1} = \sum_{j=i+1}^N L_j$.
So $f(A) = \sum_{i=1}^N A_i \cdot 10^{\sum_{j=i+1}^N L_j}$.

We need to compute $\sum_{P \in S_N} f(P) \pmod{998244353}$.
Let $P = (P_1, P_2, \ldots, P_N)$ be a permutation of $(1, 2, \ldots, N)$.
The value $f(P)$ depends on the digits of the numbers in the permutation.
Let $L_x$ be the number of digits in the integer $x$.
Then $f(P) = \sum_{i=1}^N P_i \cdot 10^{\sum_{j=i+1}^N L_{P_j}}$.
We want to compute $\sum_{P} \sum_{i=1}^N P_i \cdot 10^{\sum_{j=i+1}^N L_{P_j}}$.
By linearity of expectation (or just swapping sums), this is equal to
$\sum_{i=1}^N \sum_{P} P_i \cdot 10^{\sum_{j=i+1}^N L_{P_j}}$.

Let's fix the position $i$ in the permutation. We want to sum $P_i \cdot 10^{\sum_{j=i+1}^N L_{P_j}}$ over all permutations $P$.
The term $P_i$ is the value at position $i$.
The exponent $\sum_{j=i+1}^N L_{P_j}$ is the sum of lengths of the numbers placed at positions $i+1, \ldots, N$.
Let $K$ be the set of numbers $\{1, \ldots, N\}$.
For a fixed position $i$, we choose a value $v \in K$ to be at $P_i$. There are $(N-1)!$ permutations where $P_i = v$.
However, the exponent depends on the set of values $\{P_{i+1}, \ldots, P_N\}$.
Let $S_{suffix} = \{P_{i+1}, \ldots, P_N\}$. This is a subset of $K \setminus \{v\}$ of size $N-i$.
The exponent is $\sum_{x \in S_{suffix}} L_x$.
So for a fixed $i$ and a fixed $v$, the contribution is $v \cdot \sum_{S \subset K \setminus \{v\}, |S|=N-i} 10^{\sum_{x \in S} L_x} \cdot (N-i)! \cdot (i-1)!$.
